{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, apple cider vinegar (ACV) is acidic, but it's often recommended for acid reflux due to several mechanisms. Counterintuitively, acid reflux can sometimes be caused by *too little* stomach acid, not too much. In such cases, ACV introduces more acid, which helps to properly digest food and signals the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to close fully, preventing acid from flowing back up into the esophagus.\n\nWhile ACV is acidic, its acetic acid is a weaker acid compared to the stomach's hydrochloric acid, and it can help balance stomach acid production. Furthermore, despite its acidic nature, raw apple cider vinegar has an alkalizing effect on the body once metabolized, helping to balance the body's pH.\n\nTo use it, it's generally recommended to dilute 1 to 2 tablespoons of apple cider vinegar in a glass of water and consume it before meals. This also helps prevent potential damage to tooth enamel due to its acidity.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:2": 0.8235769271850586, "clueweb22-en0042-89-04330:9": 0.8068729639053345, "clueweb22-en0032-91-00840:17": 0.7845808267593384, "clueweb22-en0021-32-06040:8": 0.7550975680351257, "clueweb22-en0025-26-18393:8": 0.7452452778816223, "clueweb22-en0019-74-15565:5": 0.7344166040420532, "clueweb22-en0029-29-00150:12": 0.7243241667747498}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0038-17-12281:10": 0.9110813140869141, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:3": 0.9046022295951843, "clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": 0.8663996458053589, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:2": 0.8235769271850586, "clueweb22-en0042-89-04330:9": 0.8068729639053345, "clueweb22-en0035-94-16024:4": 0.8038772940635681, "clueweb22-en0038-17-12281:9": 0.7941930294036865, "clueweb22-en0032-91-00840:17": 0.7845808267593384, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:4": 0.773281455039978, "clueweb22-en0000-57-08632:4": 0.7714