{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) and H2 blockers both reduce stomach acid but work differently.\n\n**Mechanism:**\n*   **PPIs** (e.g., Omeprazole, Esomeprazole) are more potent. They block the \"proton pump\" (H+/K+ ATPase), the final step in acid production in the stomach wall, essentially turning off many of the pumps that produce acid. This is an irreversible inhibition, leading to more complete acid suppression.\n*   **H2 blockers** (e.g., Cimetidine, Famotidine) work by blocking histamine receptors, which are signals that tell your body to produce acid.\n\n**Effectiveness and Use:**\n*   **PPIs** are generally favored and considered more effective for severe or chronic acid reflux and GERD. They provide more complete and sustained acid suppression and are better at healing conditions like erosive esophagitis. They are often reserved for patients who fail H2 blocker therapy or have frequent/severe symptoms.\n*   **H2 blockers** offer less intense acid suppression. While they can be used for acute symptoms, they may become less effective if used for more than a few days in a row and rarely provide complete relief for chronic heartburn. They are a less costly option and can be used for less severe symptoms or on-demand relief.\n\n**Onset and Duration:**\n*   **H2 blockers** have a faster onset, typically relieving symptoms in 15-30 minutes, and control acid for up to 12 hours.\n*   **PPIs** have a slower onset, taking 1-4 days for their full effect, but provide sustained relief for 24 hours.\n\n**Considerations:**\n*   Prolonged use of **PPIs** has been associated with concerns such as reduced effectiveness of ingested pathogens, potential malabsorption of certain nutrients (like calcium and magnesium), and an increased risk of hip fractures, though more research is ongoing.\n*   The main drawback of **H2 blockers** is their reduced effectiveness with continuous use.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0027-58-06566:11": 0.9005780220031738, "clueweb22-en0027-58-06566:12": 0.883210301399231, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 0.8722226023674011, "clueweb22-en0001-93-13662:6": 0.8672469258308411, "clueweb22-en0039-93-00517:10": 0.8615755438804626, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:9": 0.8504967093467712, "clueweb22-en0046-25-11674:5": 0.8426214456558228, "clueweb22-en0032-45-03257:16": 0.8350355625152588, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:4": 0.8229219317436218, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:4": 0.8148589134216309, "clueweb22-en0003-55-00357:2": 0.8101412057876587, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7": 0.8073611855506897, "clueweb22-en0041-39-13388:3": 0.7981679439544678, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:6": 0.7753840088844299, "clueweb22-en0003-55-00357:1": 0.7743315100669861, "clueweb22-en0024-51-00969:0": 0.7728132009506226, "clueweb22-en0009-98-01666:8": 0.7705860137939453, "clueweb22-en0035-42-00345:14": 0.764365553855896}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0027-58-06566:11": 0.9005780220031738, "clueweb22-en0027-58-06566:12": 0.883210301399231, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 0.8722226023674011, "clueweb22-en0001-93-13662:6": 0.8672469258308411, "clueweb22-en0039-93-00517:10": 0.8615755438804626, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:9": 0.8504967093467712, "clueweb22-en0046-25-11674:5": 0.8426214456558228, "clueweb22-en0032-45-03257:16": 0.8350355625152588, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:4": 0.8229219317436218, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:4": 0.8148589134216309, "clueweb22-en0003-55-00357:2": 0.8101412057876587, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7": 0.8073611855506897, "clueweb22-en0041-40-16193:2": 0.7997103333473206, "clueweb22-en0041-39-13388:3": 0.7981679439544678, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:6": 0.7753840088844299, "clueweb22-en0003-55-00357:1": 0.7743315100669861, "clueweb22-en0024-51-00969:0": 0.7728132009506226, "clueweb22-en0033-36-18054:3": 0.7715687155723572, "clueweb22-en0009-98-01666:8": 0.7705860137939453,