{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Both proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) and H2 blockers are types of acid-suppressing medications, but they work differently to reduce stomach acid.\n\nProton pump inhibitors (PPIs) work by directly blocking the proton pumps (H+/K+ ATPase enzyme system) located in the parietal cells of the stomach wall. These pumps are responsible for the final step in the production of stomach acid. By inhibiting these pumps, PPIs effectively turn off the acid-producing mechanism, significantly reducing acid secretion.\n\nH2 blockers, on the other hand, reduce stomach acid by blocking histamine. Histamine is a substance that signals stomach cells to produce acid. By blocking the H2 (histamine-2) receptors on these cells, H2 blockers prevent them from being stimulated to secrete excess gastric acid.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-25-11674:5": 0.9441174268722534, "clueweb22-en0039-93-00517:10": 0.9380077719688416, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 0.9146270155906677, "clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 0.906991183757782, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:4": 0.8927503228187561, "clueweb22-en0001-93-13662:6": 0.8903869390487671, "clueweb22-en0003-55-00357:1": 0.8885856866836548, "clueweb22-en0015-79-03450:0": 0.8871033191680908}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0001-77-05768:12": 0.9452641606330872, "clueweb22-en0046-25-11674:5": 0.9441174268722534, "clueweb22-en0027-58-06566:11": 0.9382279515266418, "clueweb22-en0039-93-00517:10": 0.9380077719688416, "clueweb22-en0032-45-03257:16": 0.9300376176834106, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 0.9146270155906677, "clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 0.906991183757782, "clueweb22-en0040-99-17751:20": 0.8987842798233032, "clueweb22-en0009-98-01666:8": 0.8987805247306824, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:4": 0.8927503228187561, "clueweb22-en0041-39-13388:3": 0.8926578164100647, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7