{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Sourness in coffee is often a sign of under-extraction, meaning not enough flavor compounds have been dissolved from the coffee grounds. This typically occurs when the coffee doesn't have enough contact time with the water or the grind size isn't optimal.\n\nTo prevent your coffee from tasting sour:\n*   **Adjust Grind Size:** If your coffee tastes sour, your grind might be too coarse. A coarse grind allows water to pass through too quickly, leading to under-extraction. Try grinding your beans finer to ensure a more balanced extraction.\n*   **Increase Brew Time:** For brewing methods like pour-over or drip coffee, extending the brew time slightly can help achieve a fuller extraction and reduce sourness.\n*   **Consider Roast Type:** A lighter roast, especially with a coarse grind, can sometimes contribute to sourness. A darker roast might be a better choice if you consistently experience a sour taste.\n*   **Adjust Coffee-to-Water Ratio (for Cold Brew):** If you're making cold brew, using a higher coffee-to-water ratio (more coffee, less water) can help prevent sourness.\n*   **Add a Pinch of Salt:** A small amount of salt can help counteract sourness in coffee and enhance its overall flavor.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:15": 0.968755304813385, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:18": 0.9594559669494629, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:14": 0.9531363248825073, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:19": 0.9499800801277161, "clueweb22-en0002-51-06448:22": 0.9429993033409119, "clueweb22-en0004-93-04535:13": 0.9389153718948364, "clueweb22-en0000-23-14012:2": 0.9201293587684631, "clueweb22-en0004-93-04535:11": 0.9072285294532776}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0029-18-03900:1": 0.9877132177352905, "clueweb22-en0029-18-03900:3": 0.982584536075592, "clueweb22-en0029-18-03900:2": 0.9781362414360046, "clueweb22-e