{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The taste of coffee is influenced by a wide range of factors, starting long before it reaches your cup. Key elements include the **origin and growing conditions** of the beans, such as the specific plant varietal, soil composition, elevation, climate, and the amount of rain and sunshine it receives. Higher elevations, for instance, can lead to more complex flavors due to slower bean maturation.\n\n**Harvesting and processing methods** also play a crucial role. Ripe, handpicked beans generally yield better flavor than unripe ones or those harvested mechanically. The post-harvest processing, including fermentation, significantly impacts the final sensory quality.\n\nFurthermore, the **roasting degree** causes intricate physical and chemical changes in the beans, directly affecting the beverage's quality. After roasting, the **freshness of the beans** and their storage conditions are vital, as oxidation can lead to staleness.\n\nFinally, your **brewing method** heavily influences the taste. Factors like the **grind size**, water temperature, and extraction rate (avoiding over or under-extraction) are critical. Using fresh beans and appropriate equipment also contributes to a great-tasting cup.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-45-06138:1": 0.9938145875930786, "clueweb22-en0033-49-03668:5": 0.993722140789032, "clueweb22-en0035-10-05441:1": 0.9756363034248352, "clueweb22-en0012-45-06138:2": 0.9753045439720154, "clueweb22-en0038-48-07837:3": 0.9681785702705383, "clueweb22-en0007-86-14667:2": 0.9619094133377075, "clueweb22-en0000-74-13886:3": 0.9519348740577698, "clueweb22-en0007-86-14667:3": 0.9476202130317688, "clueweb22-en0033-49-03668:6": 0.9415031671524048, "clueweb22-en0034-86-14025:1": 0.9407625198364258, "clueweb22-en0024-49-11656:2": 0.9381868243217468, "clueweb22-en0015-62-16728:1": 0.92879718542099, "clueweb22-en