{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Roasting coffee beans dramatically transforms them from green, bland, and acidic to flavorful and aromatic. This process involves significant physical and chemical changes. Physically, beans lose moisture, causing them to lose weight (typically 12-20%) and expand in volume (50-100% larger). Their density decreases, and porosity increases, making them more soluble for brewing. The internal pressure changes the cell walls, and oils migrate to the surface, contributing to the bean's appearance and flavor.\n\nChemically, roasting kickstarts complex reactions like the Maillard reaction, caramelization, and Strecker degradation. These reactions develop diverse flavors and aromas, converting natural sugars and amino acids into compounds responsible for sweetness, fruity acidity, and a richer profile. Unroasted beans have a distinctive grassy, bitter, and acidic taste. Roasting reduces acidity, especially in darker roasts.\n\nThe roast level also impacts caffeine content; light and medium roasts retain more caffeine than darker roasts. After roasting, beans begin to release trapped carbon dioxide in a process called \"degassing.\" While this gas contributes to flavor, its gradual escape leads to flavor loss over time if the coffee is not consumed fresh.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0041-01-05927:8": 0.9606868624687195, "clueweb22-en0041-01-05927:7": 0.9176057577133179, "clueweb22-en0024-55-10319:8": 0.8926396369934082, "clueweb22-en0022-32-07211:5": 0.8832255005836487, "clueweb22-en0010-13-03035:6": 0.8782039880752563, "clueweb22-en0040-04-13369:2": 0.863287091255188, "clueweb22-en0041-01-05927:5": 0.8628567457199097, "clueweb22-en0001-58-05338:1": 0.8445751667022705, "clueweb22-en0026-82-04612:8": 0.8352118134498596, "clueweb22-en0014-31-04534:8": 0.8313908576965332, "clueweb22-en0007-62-04227:3": 0.8273162841796875, "clue