{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When experiencing finger pain, the characteristics can vary depending on whether it's a pulley, joint, or tendon injury, especially in climbing.\n\nFor **pulley injuries**, often a loud \"pop\" is heard, followed by significant swelling and pain. For A2 pulley injuries, common in climbers, the pain usually arises at the base of the finger and is noticeable when trying to straighten or bend it. Pain is typically on the front of the finger. Severe ruptures (A2-A4) may show \"bowstringing\" \u2013 a bulge at the base of the finger. The pain can be sharp or dull and hurts when squeezing or using the injured finger.\n\n**Joint injuries** like ligament or capsular strains usually cause pain on the side of the joint rather than the front. Sprains can lead to an unstable or wobbly joint, swelling, pain, bruising, numbness, tingling, or a grating sound with movement. If it's arthritis, you might feel stiffness and a dull, aching pain, often worsened by grip and pinch activities, especially in the mornings. Swelling, grinding sensations, warmth, redness, and even bony nodules or deformities can also occur.\n\n**Tendon injuries** can limit how much the finger can bend or flex, causing pain when attempting to move it. Common signs include tenderness along the palm side of your hand and sometimes numbness in the fingertip. Tendonitis, or tendon inflammation, results in increased friction and pain with movement due to swelling of the tendons or thickening of their sheath. Similar to pulley injuries, the pain can be sharp or dull and hurts when squeezing the area or using the injured finger.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 0.48035353422164917, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.44092613458633423, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.41981562972068787, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 0.3853088319301605, "clueweb22-en0015-71