{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, it is possible that your pain in the front of your finger, especially when crimping, could still be related to an A2 pulley injury, even if you don't observe any bowstringing.\n\nA2 pulley injuries are very common among climbers, and pain on the front or base of the finger is a typical symptom. Crimping significantly increases the forces exerted on the A2 pulley, making it a common mechanism for this type of injury.\n\nWhile bowstringing\u2014a visible bulging at the base of the finger\u2014can occur with more severe A2-A4 pulley ruptures, its absence doesn't rule out an A2 pulley injury. Bowstringing is a sign of a more serious tear and is often diagnosed clinically or with imaging by a professional. It's possible to have a pulley strain or less severe injury without this prominent visual sign.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.7550577521324158, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 0.5166044235229492, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.34778448939323425, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:18": 0.21749569475650787, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": 0.16371116042137146}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 0.7550577521324158, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": 0.5166044235229492, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 0.34778448939323425, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:2": 0.30813780426979065, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:3": 0.29602986574172974, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 0.28622347116470337, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.2430848926305771, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:18": 0.21749569475650787, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:4": 0.20965757966041565, "clueweb22-en0018-88-13146:5": 0.20616669952869415, "clueweb22-en0018-88-13146:2": 0.19884008169174194, "clueweb22-en0018-88-13146:6": 0.1944863349199295, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 0.19412730634212494, "clueweb22-en0000-18-09663:9": 0.19403822720