{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For supporting your A2 pulley when returning to climbing, H-taping is highly recommended as research shows it's effective in keeping tendons close to the bone and can even increase crimping strength.\n\nTo apply H-taping:\n1.  Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape (like Leukoplast brand) about 10cm long and 1.5cm wide.\n2.  Cut the tape lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm bridge in the middle.\n3.  Place the tape on your injured finger with the bridge on the inside, sitting on the crease of the joint on the upper end of the injured A2 pulley.\n4.  Flex your finger 60\u00b0 at the joint being taped.\n5.  Wrap the upper part of the tape around your finger to form a ring.\n6.  Then, wrap the other part of the tape below the joint to form a second ring.\n\nIt's crucial to retape your finger between each climb, as the tape stretches quickly and its support will diminish. Some experts, like Dr. Julian Saunders, suggest an alternative method where the tape is applied to completely restrict finger bending, especially when crimping, to ensure no further damage. However, H-taping offers support while allowing more functional movement.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.9294650554656982, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 0.8805570006370544, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.8745455145835876, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.8224632740020752, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 0.7866395711898804, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.6933505535125732, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 0.6601141095161438, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 0.6437700390815735, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 0.6326256990432739}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 0.9581419825553894, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.9294650554656982, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 0.8805570006370544, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7":