{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "H-taping is a highly effective method for supporting injured finger pulleys, especially for climbers. Research by Doctors Isabelle and Viktor Sch\u00f6ffl shows it's more effective than other taping methods like X-taping or circular taping in keeping tendons close to the bone and preventing bowstringing, particularly when crimping. It can also increase the crimping strength of injured fingers by an average of 13%.\n\nTo apply H-taping:\n1.  Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape (like Leukoplast) about 10cm long and 1.5cm wide.\n2.  Cut the tape in half lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm bridge in the middle.\n3.  Place the tape on the injured finger with the bridge positioned on the inside of the finger, sitting on the crease of the joint above the injured pulley.\n4.  Flex your finger 60 degrees at the joint you are taping.\n5.  Wrap the upper part of the tape around the finger to form a ring, then wrap the other part of the tape below the joint to form a second ring.\n\nRemember to retape your finger between climbs or whenever you feel the tape slackening, as tape can stretch quickly and lose its support. This method is for recovery and support, not for preventing injuries in healthy fingers.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 0.861500084400177, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.8386898040771484, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 0.6692042946815491, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 0.5500901341438293, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 0.503662645816803, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 0.46119269728660583, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:14": 0.43869972229003906, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 0.4213363528251648, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 0.3141699731349945}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 0.861500084400177, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 0.8386898040771484, "clueweb22