{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-2_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Since biking isn't quite what you're looking for, there are several other excellent low-impact sports you could try while recovering from your climbing injury.\n\n**Swimming** and **deep-water running** are fantastic options. They are gentle on your joints, provide a full-body workout, and the water supports your body, making them ideal for injury recovery without putting stress on your hands or fingers.\n\n**Walking** is another simple yet effective choice. It's low-impact, easily accessible, and can be done outdoors in pleasant weather or indoors. You can vary your routes to keep it interesting.\n\nFor a different kind of full-body workout, consider **rowing**. It's a great way to engage your legs, core, and upper body, and can be done indoors on a machine, minimizing impact.\n\nAdditionally, you could focus on **upper-body exercises** like dumbbell presses, shoulder presses, or lat pulldowns. These will help you maintain strength and fitness without putting direct pressure on your healing finger, preparing you for when you can return to climbing.\n\nThese activities can help you stay active, boost your mood, and support your recovery.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0042-15-06938:2": 0.9208841323852539, "clueweb22-en0029-21-19574:12": 0.9047552943229675, "clueweb22-en0014-84-12611:17": 0.8189244270324707, "clueweb22-en0032-21-01420:2": 0.7806637287139893, "clueweb22-en0031-88-16339:6": 0.7775028944015503, "clueweb22-en0008-81-15166:1": 0.7746305465698242, "clueweb22-en0008-58-15028:12": 0.772521436214447, "clueweb22-en0028-50-02726:4": 0.7714361548423767, "clueweb22-en0033-73-04561:4": 0.7577810883522034}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0042-15-06938:2": 0.9208841323852539, "clueweb22-en0029-21-19574:12": 0.9047552943229675, "clueweb22-en0002-70-03092:5": 0.8982133269309998, "clueweb22-en0004-30