{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "That's a great goal! Preparing for a 10-mile run, especially if you're not very confident in your fitness, is definitely achievable with the right approach.\n\nThe most important step is to choose a suitable training plan for beginners. Many 10-mile training plans, often 10 to 12 weeks long, are available and designed to gradually build your endurance. These plans typically include:\n*   **Long Runs:** Progressively increasing your mileage each week, often on weekends.\n*   **Maintenance Runs:** Shorter runs to keep up weekly mileage and focus on form.\n*   **Cross-Training:** Activities like yoga, strength training, biking, or swimming are crucial to strengthen muscles, prevent injuries, and improve form.\n*   **Rest Days:** Essential for recovery and preventing fatigue.\n\nPacing yourself is key; aim for a comfortable, conversational pace on most runs. For runs over 60 minutes, remember to fuel your body with snacks like fruit, nuts, or energy gels, and stay hydrated with water or sports drinks. Post-run, a mini-meal with protein, fat, and carbs (like egg or tuna salad) helps with recovery. Don't forget essential gear like proper running shoes, a portable water bottle, and a foam roller for muscle soreness. Mentally breaking up the distance during your runs can also help manage the challenge.\n\nBy following a structured plan and listening to your body, you'll be well on your way to completing your 10-mile run!", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-07-06552:3": 0.9825604557991028, "clueweb22-en0004-89-17215:4": 0.968835175037384, "clueweb22-en0025-82-01264:1": 0.9616060853004456, "clueweb22-en0019-07-06552:2": 0.9530981779098511, "clueweb22-en0026-55-13129:14": 0.9464441537857056, "clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:11": 0.9363812804222107, "clueweb22-en0012-01-07241:5": 0.9347354173660278, "clueweb22-en0026-55-13129:15": 0.925