{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Determining your lactate threshold (LT) helps you understand the fastest pace you can sustain without rapidly accumulating lactic acid and fatigue, which is crucial for improving endurance.\n\nOne common and practical way to estimate your lactate threshold is through a **20-minute time trial**:\n1.  Warm up for 10-15 minutes with easy jogging.\n2.  Then, run at the fastest pace you can sustain for 20 minutes.\n3.  If you have a heart rate monitor, record your average heart rate during the last 20 minutes of this run. This average heart rate is an estimate of your heart rate at lactate threshold.\n\nAlternatively, you can do a **30-minute treadmill test**:\n1.  Set the treadmill at a 1% grade and warm up for 10 minutes.\n2.  Run for 30 minutes at the fastest pace you can maintain.\n3.  Note your heart rate at 10 minutes and 30 minutes, then average them. This average is your estimated LT heart rate. Your average pace during this run is your approximate LT pace.\n\nYou can also estimate it by running at a 'comfortably hard' pace, which feels like an 8-8.5 out of 10 effort, or corresponds to heart rate Zone 4. Some GPS devices like Garmin can also estimate your lactate threshold by analyzing your heart rate and pace data during runs. For the most accurate measurement, a lab test with a sports scientist is recommended.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0028-06-06729:11": 0.9868993759155273, "clueweb22-en0015-53-11613:1": 0.9794554710388184, "clueweb22-en0025-86-10537:4": 0.9701927900314331, "clueweb22-en0035-06-16993:8": 0.9642305374145508, "clueweb22-en0021-36-12745:2": 0.9609134197235107, "clueweb22-en0004-71-03439:5": 0.9594172239303589, "clueweb22-en0033-86-01487:3": 0.9572193622589111, "clueweb22-en0046-47-18551:18": 0.9504933953285217, "clueweb22-en0021-36-12745:3": 0.9501954913139343}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "referen