{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Choosing the right running shoes is crucial for comfort, performance, and injury prevention. The most important factor is **comfort** \u2013 the shoes should feel good from the moment you try them on, like an extension of your feet, and should not suddenly become comfortable later.\n\nIt's highly recommended to get your shoes **professionally fitted** at a running specialty store or by a podiatrist. When trying them on, wear the socks you intend to run in and jog around the store to assess the feel. Running shoes are often half to a full size larger than your casual shoes and should not be too snug, allowing your foot to splay upon impact. Always fit to your larger foot.\n\nKey features to look for include:\n*   **Support and Stability:** This is vital for your foot type (neutral, low arch, high arch) and running style (e.g., overpronation). Shoes offer different levels of control, cushioning, and stability.\n*   **Cushioning:** Provides shock absorption and comfort. You can choose from lightweight (minimal cushion) to maximum cushioning, depending on your preference and body weight.\n*   **Flexibility:** The shoe should bend easily at the ball of your foot.\n*   **Lightweight:** Generally preferred for endurance and speed.\n*   **Durability:** Ensure the materials are tough enough for long mileage.\n\nRemember, never use a brand new pair of shoes for a race. They should be broken in for at least 100 miles beforehand.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0026-47-01829:12": 0.9813516736030579, "clueweb22-en0032-57-03681:10": 0.9804701209068298, "clueweb22-en0029-41-08595:8": 0.9678630828857422, "clueweb22-en0032-57-03681:11": 0.9661450982093811, "clueweb22-en0026-47-01829:13": 0.965238094329834, "clueweb22-en0040-62-14515:1": 0.9596148133277893, "clueweb22-en0026-47-01829:11": 0.9595025181770325, "clueweb22-en0004-12-10209:10