{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "New York City offers numerous excellent spots for training, regardless of your fitness level.\n\nFor running, Central Park is a fantastic choice. It features a challenging 6-mile loop with hills, like Harlem Hill, but also offers shorter routes around The Reservoir or other paths, making it suitable for both casual joggers and more serious runners. You'll be surrounded by a diverse community of athletes, which can be very motivating.\n\nIf you prefer indoor training or a variety of classes, New York has a wide array of gyms and fitness studios. Major chains like Crunch and New York Sports Clubs have multiple locations across Manhattan and Brooklyn, offering extensive group fitness classes including yoga, Pilates, spinning, and strength-training sessions. For a more high-end experience, consider Equinox or the Sports Center at Chelsea Piers, which boast areas for running, rock climbing, basketball, and swimming, alongside many classes.\n\nYou can also explore specific fitness classes tailored to different interests. Websites like ClassPass can help you discover studios offering everything from cycling and dance to boxing and martial arts, with many options available for beginners. For something unique, you might try rock climbing at places like Brooklyn Boulders or participate in an outdoor bootcamp like C.A.M.P. XT in Central Park, which incorporates resistance training and drills.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0043-74-03148:0": 0.7022294402122498, "clueweb22-en0044-98-18570:11": 0.6798816323280334, "clueweb22-en0005-09-18970:1": 0.6768277287483215, "clueweb22-en0008-95-04168:0": 0.6465845108032227, "clueweb22-en0021-36-15243:0": 0.644245445728302, "clueweb22-en0005-09-18970:0": 0.6380555033683777, "clueweb22-en0006-33-06834:5": 0.6346830129623413, "clueweb22-en0008-95-04168:1": 0.6330918669700623, "clueweb22-en0040-