{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Stevia, xylitol, erythritol, and yacon syrup are generally considered healthier alternatives to white sugar, with Stevia often ranked highest. Natural sugars like maple syrup, molasses, and honey are less harmful but should be used sparingly.\n\n**Stevia** is a zero-calorie, plant-based sweetener much sweeter than sugar. It can lower blood pressure and blood sugar, benefiting diabetics, and has antioxidants. Side effects like bloating are possible.\n\n**Xylitol**, a sugar alcohol, is similar in sweetness to sugar but with 40% fewer calories and doesn't raise blood sugar, aiding diabetics. It supports dental and bone health, but excessive intake may cause digestive issues.\n\n**Erythritol**, another sugar alcohol, is 70% as sweet as sugar with very few calories. It's well-tolerated digestively and has no significant negative side effects.\n\n**Yacon syrup** aids weight loss and digestion due to antioxidants and fiber, and is beneficial for diabetes, though it can cause intestinal distress and isn't for high-heat cooking.\n\n**Honey, maple syrup, and molasses** offer some nutrients and antioxidants, unlike white sugar. Honey and maple syrup are sweeter than sugar, while molasses is less sweet. All are high in calories and carbs. Maple syrup has a lower glycemic index and more antioxidants than white sugar and honey. Molasses has high antioxidant levels and minerals but is calorie-dense and not for diabetics. Honey is high in antioxidants but not ideal for diabetics; raw honey can be unsafe for infants. Moderation is crucial for all.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-54-01249:7": 0.7862802147865295, "clueweb22-en0029-79-06560:9": 0.7850396037101746, "clueweb22-en0036-03-10430:13": 0.6839311718940735, "clueweb22-en0026-66-11327:17": 0.6548343300819397, "clueweb22-en0026-66-11327:16": 0.6435787677764893, "clueweb22-en002