{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, you will need to adjust your diet during pregnancy to support both your health and your baby's development. The general advice is to focus on a healthy, balanced, and varied diet rich in nutrient-dense, whole foods, rather than \"eating for two.\"\n\nYour calorie needs will increase gradually: no extra calories are typically needed in the first trimester, about 300-350 extra per day in the second, and around 500 extra in the third.\n\nKey dietary changes and recommendations include:\n*   **Eat a variety of foods:** Include plenty of whole grains (like brown rice, whole wheat bread/pasta), fruits, vegetables, lean proteins (meat, poultry, fish, beans, tofu), and dairy products or calcium-fortified alternatives.\n*   **Increase specific nutrients:** Aim for 1000-1300 mg of calcium, 27 mg of iron, and 220 mcg of iodine daily. Taking a prenatal vitamin supplement, including folic acid (400 mcg daily until week 12) and vitamin D, is strongly recommended.\n*   **Boost fiber intake:** Around 25-35 grams daily from whole grains, fruits, vegetables, and beans can help with digestion and prevent constipation.\n*   **Stay hydrated:** Drink about 10 cups (2 liters) of water daily.\n*   **Foods to limit/avoid:** Steer clear of raw or undercooked meats, eggs, and seafood; unpasteurized dairy and juices; high-mercury fish (like shark, swordfish); excessive salt; and sugary foods/drinks. Limit caffeine to under 200 mg per day. Avoid carbohydrate-restrictive diets like keto.\n\nAlways discuss your diet and any supplements with your doctor or a dietitian to ensure it meets your individual needs.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-41-08982:10": 0.9768721461296082, "clueweb22-en0018-28-09879:1": 0.9701356887817383, "clueweb22-en0027-79-08948:2": 0.9700286388397217, "clueweb22-en0044-57-08176:5": 0.967968225479126, "clueweb22-en0008-