{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, consuming alcohol while breastfeeding can indeed reduce your milk supply and affect your baby's intake. Studies show that alcohol disrupts the balance of hormones, such as prolactin and oxytocin, which are crucial for milk production and the let-down reflex. This means that alcohol can hinder both the production and release of breast milk.\n\nEven a small amount, like a single beer or glass of wine, can temporarily reduce your milk supply and disrupt these milk-producing hormones. Babies tend to drink less milk in the hours after their mother has consumed alcohol, with some studies indicating a 20-23% decrease in milk volume, even if they nurse more frequently. This can also be due to alcohol altering the taste of your milk.\n\nContrary to popular belief, drinking beer does not increase milk supply. Regular or heavy alcohol consumption can lead to a more significant and sustained decrease in milk supply, potentially affecting your baby's growth and leading to a shorter duration of breastfeeding.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-16983:0": 0.9556519985198975, "clueweb22-en0002-39-11926:5": 0.9393941164016724, "clueweb22-en0019-08-09434:1": 0.9253653287887573, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 0.9112891554832458, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:8": 0.8965374827384949, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:12": 0.8822393417358398, "clueweb22-en0005-68-03159:4": 0.8769136071205139, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:9": 0.873637318611145}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-16983:0": 0.9556519985198975, "clueweb22-en0002-39-11926:5": 0.9393941164016724, "clueweb22-en0042-99-06708:12": 0.9322140216827393, "clueweb22-en0019-08-09434:1": 0.9253653287887573, "clueweb22-en0019-08-09434:2": 0.922503650188446, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 0.9112891554832458, "clueweb22-en0024-10-07042:1": 0.9047548174858093, "clueweb