{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Congratulations again! Beyond diet, there's a lot more to know for a healthy pregnancy and successful breastfeeding journey.\n\nFor **nutrition**, your energy needs increase: by 200-300 calories daily during pregnancy and up to 500 calories or more while breastfeeding. Staying well-hydrated is also crucial. Ensure you're getting enough essential nutrients like folate (important for your baby's brain and spinal cord development), calcium (1000 mg/day for strong bones and teeth), and iron. Your doctor may recommend a daily prenatal multivitamin with iron. However, always consult your healthcare professional before taking any supplements, as large amounts can be harmful, and be mindful of interactions with medicines.\n\n**Breastfeeding offers numerous benefits for both you and your baby.** For you, it can help your uterus return to its pre-pregnancy size, aid in weight loss, and may reduce your risk of breast, ovarian, uterine, and endometrial cancers, as well as conditions like diabetes, osteoporosis, high blood pressure, high cholesterol, stroke, and heart disease. For your baby, breastfeeding strengthens their immune system, protecting them from infections like diarrhea, ear infections, and reducing the risk of Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS), allergies, asthma, and later-life conditions like type-2 diabetes and obesity.\n\nPractically, **support is key for breastfeeding**. Lactation consultants can help with correct latching techniques, various breastfeeding positions, and address concerns like clogged ducts or sore nipples. They can also advise on medication safety while nursing, managing multiple babies, or stimulating milk production. When caring for your newborn, remember to learn their feeding cues (\"watch your baby, not the clock\") and seek guidance on safe sleep positions, holding, diapering, and bathin