{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your concerns about dark spots, uneven tone, and visible pores, and your desire for non-serum solutions with minimal downtime, here are some recommendations that complement your current routine:\n\nFor **dark spots, uneven tone, and visible pores**, consider incorporating **Retinol creams**. These formulations, unlike serums, can effectively promote skin cell turnover, reduce the appearance of dark spots, even out skin tone, and visibly minimize pore size (Passages 4, 9, 16). Some retinol creams also contain niacinamide, which further strengthens the skin barrier and improves overall radiance (Passage 4).\n\nTo enhance your **Vitamin C** benefits in a non-serum format, you can use **L-Ascorbic Acid powder**. This pure form of Vitamin C can be mixed into your existing creams or moisturizers, providing fresh antioxidant power to brighten your complexion, reduce dark spots, and address uneven tone (Passages 1, 19).\n\nAdditionally, look for **creams infused with Niacinamide** (Vitamin B3) and **collagen**. Niacinamide helps improve brightness, suppleness, and reduces dark spots and the appearance of pores by strengthening the skin's protective barrier (Passage 9). Collagen creams can also promote cell turnover, reducing age spots and enlarged pores (Passage 9).\n\nFor gentle exfoliation and brightening, **buttermilk** is noted for its lactic acid content, which can help fade dark spots (Passage 8). For daily cleansing, **face washes or cleansers containing charcoal and witch hazel** can help remove impurities and give pores a firmer appearance (Passage 9).\n\nAlways check the ingredient list of any new product to ensure it is safe for your nut allergy.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0030-10-09769:5": 0.8625017404556274, "clueweb22-en0019-02-16513:9": 0.8135102987289429, "clueweb22-en0043-03-08911:3": 0.7995052337646