{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "CO2 laser treatment works by generating a specific wavelength of light from electricity passing through a gas-filled tube. This light is emitted in short, concentrated beams that target water molecules in the skin, vaporizing surface skin cells (epidermis) to remove damaged outer layers and reveal fresh skin underneath. Simultaneously, the laser penetrates the deeper dermis layer, heating it to stimulate the production of new collagen and elastin fibers. This collagen remodeling helps to firm, smooth, and tighten the skin over time.\n\nThere are two main types: fully ablative and fractional. Fully ablative CO2 lasers remove the entire superficial skin layer in the treated area, triggering a regenerative healing response. Fractional CO2 lasers, on the other hand, deliver a partial beam of light in a pixelated pattern, creating thousands of tiny shafts that penetrate deeper. The skin then repairs these shafts by pushing out old, damaged skin and replacing it with new, while the heat also helps shrink existing collagen. This fractional approach removes only a portion of the skin, leading to less aggressive treatments.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:1": 0.9988401532173157, "clueweb22-en0040-62-12136:1": 0.9357315897941589, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 0.9174416065216064, "clueweb22-en0018-53-08940:14": 0.8843122124671936, "clueweb22-en0032-14-04213:8": 0.8533777594566345, "clueweb22-en0002-32-13360:17": 0.8283112645149231}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:1": 0.9988401532173157, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:13": 0.9831081032752991, "clueweb22-en0025-02-00163:1": 0.9712554216384888, "clueweb22-en0008-96-16607:2": 0.9463271498680115, "clueweb22-en0037-37-03816:4": 0.9376450777053833, "clueweb22-en0040-62-12136:1": 0.9357315897941589, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:2": 0.928441584