{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The main difference between fractional CO2 laser treatment and fully ablative CO2 laser treatment lies in how much skin they treat and remove.\n\n**Fully ablative CO2 lasers** completely remove the entire superficial layer of skin in the treated area. This process is quite intense, often requiring general anesthesia. Due to the extensive removal, the downtime is typically longer, a minimum of two weeks, during which the skin can be swollen, scabbed, and raw. While highly effective, one treatment often suffices for desired results, and they are generally recommended only for fair or light skin tones due to a higher risk of pigmentation issues.\n\n**Fractional CO2 lasers**, on the other hand, deliver a partial beam of light that targets only a *fraction* of the skin in a pixelated pattern. This creates microscopic columns of treated tissue while leaving surrounding skin intact, allowing for faster healing. These treatments are considerably less aggressive, usually requiring only local anesthesia. Downtime is significantly shorter, often just a matter of days. However, most people need a series of treatments to achieve optimal results. Fractional CO2 is a newer technology with minimized pain, downtime, and risks, suitable for mild to moderate signs of aging and damage.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 0.9917844533920288, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:9": 0.934853196144104, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:12": 0.9190477132797241, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:11": 0.8953569531440735, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:13": 0.6994383931159973, "clueweb22-en0022-44-12230:2": 0.568974494934082, "clueweb22-en0037-72-10442:7": 0.5326297879219055}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 0.9917844533920288, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:9": 0.934853196144104, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:12": 0.919047713