{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_13", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Microneedling and Mesotherapy are both treatments aimed at improving skin appearance, but they work through different mechanisms.\n\nMicroneedling, also known as collagen induction therapy, involves creating precise, microscopic punctures in the skin using a device with tiny needles. These \"micro-injuries\" stimulate the skin's natural healing process, leading to increased production of collagen, elastin, and hyaluronic acid. This process helps improve skin cell turnover, resulting in brighter, smoother skin, and is effective for reducing acne scarring, stretch marks, surgical scarring, open pores, fine lines, wrinkles, pigmentation, sun damage, and a dull complexion. Microneedling has minimal downtime, with common side effects being temporary redness and inflammation, and it is suitable for most skin tones and types.\n\nMesotherapy, on the other hand, is not quite the same as microneedling. It involves actually injecting vitamins and other skin nutrients directly under the skin.\n\nBased on the information available, microneedling offers a wide range of benefits for overall skin improvement with minimal downtime. While Mesotherapy involves injecting nutrients, the provided passages do not offer detailed information on its specific benefits, effectiveness for your concerns, or potential side effects compared to microneedling.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-83-01928:7": 0.7258715033531189, "clueweb22-en0038-11-18082:1": 0.7141146063804626, "clueweb22-en0037-79-06507:1": 0.6926446557044983, "clueweb22-en0038-11-18082:2": 0.682612419128418}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0015-46-11266:19": 0.813835859298706, "clueweb22-en0016-80-15701:8": 0.7972895503044128, "clueweb22-en0010-04-11094:4": 0.7611618638038635, "clueweb22-en0004-83-01928:7": 0.7258715033531189, "clueweb22-en0028-08-18712:12": 0.