{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For someone with fatty liver, baking your own bread at home is highly beneficial as it gives you full control over the ingredients, allowing you to avoid harmful additives.\n\nHere are some suitable bread options:\n\n*   **Rye Bread:** This is considered one of the best choices due to its high fiber content and fewer carbs than regular white bread. It can be bitter, so mixing rye flour with whole wheat flour is recommended for a better taste. Ensure it's homemade or made with only base ingredients (flour, yeast, salt, water) to avoid added sugars or preservatives.\n*   **Whole Wheat Bread:** A good alternative to white flour, as it's not unnaturally bleached and contains more fiber, which slows sugar absorption. Opt for 100% whole wheat bread with simple, healthy ingredients.\n*   **Keto Bread:** If prepared at home, keto breads can be a good option. Store-bought versions often contain preservatives and chemicals that are not liver-friendly.\n*   **Sprouted Bread:** Made from sprouted whole grains, this type offers more nutrients, lower carb counts, and doesn't increase blood sugar levels as much as other breads.\n*   **Other Whole Grain Options:** Whole-grain, multi-grain, stone ground, pita, and sourdough breads are also better choices than refined white bread.\n\nIt's crucial to avoid white bread, as its simple carbohydrates are rapidly converted to sugar, contributing to insulin resistance and fatty liver disease. Even with healthier options, moderation is key; try to limit your intake to 2-3 slices per day to support liver recovery.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:7": 0.987504780292511, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:11": 0.9835790991783142, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:2": 0.9503113031387329, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:10": 0.9313550591468811, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:6": 0.8991051912307739, "clueweb22-e