{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For fatty liver, choosing the right bread is crucial. You should avoid white bread, as its simple carbohydrates are quickly converted to sugar, contributing to insulin resistance and fatty liver disease. Many store-bought breads also contain added sugars, preservatives, and chemicals that are not liver-friendly.\n\nInstead, focus on whole-grain and sprouted options. Excellent choices include:\n*   **Rye bread:** High in fiber and lower in carbs. It can be bitter, so mixing rye flour with whole wheat flour is recommended for a better taste.\n*   **Whole wheat bread:** A good alternative to white bread, providing more fiber. Ensure it's 100% whole wheat with minimal ingredients (flour, yeast, salt, water).\n*   **Keto bread:** A good option if you bake it yourself, as store-bought versions often contain unhealthy additives.\n*   **Sprouted bread:** Made from sprouted whole grains, offering more nutrients and less impact on blood sugar levels. Brands like Ezekiel are mentioned as an option.\n*   **Other healthy whole-grain options:** Multi-grain, stone ground, pita, and sourdough breads are also suitable.\n\nRegardless of the type, consume healthy bread in moderation, ideally limiting yourself to 2-3 slices per day to support liver recovery. Prioritize baking your own bread to control ingredients and avoid harmful additives.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:7": 0.986164927482605, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:11": 0.9752006530761719, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:2": 0.9295694828033447, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:10": 0.9241794943809509, "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:5": 0.9044384956359863, "clueweb22-en0034-02-17207:12": 0.8969690799713135, "clueweb22-en0025-78-05449:1": 0.8790109157562256, "clueweb22-en0012-09-04746:11": 0.8704437017440796, "clueweb22-en0014-11-18570:3": 0.8252658843994141}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], 