{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "That's a very common experience for individuals with ADHD. The feeling of being overwhelmed by multiple tasks can lead to task-switching without completion. The key is to shift from juggling many things at once to focusing on one task at a time until it's done.\n\nHere are some strategies to help:\n1.  **Single-Task Focus:** Make a conscious effort to stick to one task until it's completed before moving to the next. Avoid starting new projects or tasks until your current one is finished.\n2.  **Avoid Overscheduling:** It's easy to take on too much, which can exacerbate feelings of overwhelm and lead to task-jumping. Practice saying 'no' to new tasks or projects if your plate is already full.\n3.  **Time Blocking & Pomodoro Technique:** Dedicate specific blocks of time (e.g., 15 or 25 minutes) to work on a single task with no distractions. Set a timer and commit to focusing only on that one task during the block. When the timer goes off, you can decide to continue or take a short break.\n4.  **Schedule Tasks:** Instead of just having a long to-do list, try scheduling specific tasks into your day. This can make them feel more attainable and reduce the urge to jump between them.\n5.  **Reward Completion:** Give yourself a small reward after successfully completing a task. This can provide an incentive and reinforce the habit of finishing what you start.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 0.9546639323234558, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:10": 0.9308351278305054, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:3": 0.9275910258293152, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:9": 0.9195034503936768, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:11": 0.919102668762207, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:5": 0.9124786853790283, "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:11": 0.9038009643554688, "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:10": 0.8472310304641724, "clueweb22-en0033-18-04361:4": 0.844525992870330