{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "I understand you're looking for strategies that support your hyperfocus rather than interrupting it. The rigid timing of the Pomodoro Technique isn't for everyone, especially when you're in a deep flow state.\n\nOne flexible alternative is the **Flowtime technique**. Instead of setting a fixed timer, you start working and track how long you naturally focus on a task without interruption. When you feel your focus wane, you take a break, and then note the duration of your focus session. Over time, this helps you identify your natural focus patterns and peak concentration times, allowing you to schedule your work blocks to align with your inherent rhythm. This way, you're working with your flow, not against it, and you can extend your work periods when you're in hyperfocus without being cut off by a timer.\n\nAnother approach you could try is the **52/17 rule**. This method suggests working for 52 minutes of focused time, followed by a 17-minute break. This longer work block might be more accommodating for your hyperfocus periods compared to the shorter 25-minute Pomodoro sessions, as it allows for a more extended period of uninterrupted concentration before a break.\n\nThese methods offer more flexibility, allowing you to work with your natural attention span and leverage your hyperfocus effectively.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:10": 0.8681815266609192, "clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:11": 0.8101892471313477, "clueweb22-en0003-35-15851:17": 0.7508648633956909}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0028-53-13098:17": 0.9100674986839294, "clueweb22-en0038-18-12276:5": 0.8705137372016907, "clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:10": 0.8681815266609192, "clueweb22-en0021-49-01341:10": 0.8590332865715027, "clueweb22-en0038-18-12276:4": 0.8364642262458801, "clueweb22-en0029-26-09570:13": 0.8209137320518494, "clue