{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To prepare for scheduled meetings and prevent them from disrupting your hyperfocus, several strategies can help you maintain your momentum.\n\nFirst, leverage your calendar. Use calendar software to set alarms well in advance\u2014perhaps an hour or two before, and then again 15 minutes prior\u2014to give yourself ample time to transition and prepare. Review your daily and weekly calendars each morning to plan your day around these fixed appointments. Receiving meeting invitations and reminders also helps you stay on track.\n\nSecond, prioritize preparation for the meeting itself. Always try to get the meeting agenda in advance. This allows you to make a list of topics and solutions to be discussed, gather any necessary materials, and mentally prepare for the discussion. Being prepared helps you stay focused during the meeting and ensures it runs efficiently.\n\nThird, integrate meetings into your work schedule using time blocking. After creating your to-do list, block out specific times in your calendar for focused work, scheduling meetings around these blocks. This helps you visually see your work periods and protects your hyperfocus. If possible, take a short break (e.g., 5-10 minutes) right before a meeting to reset and transition your focus, rather than jumping directly from deep work.\n\nFinally, advocate for well-structured meetings. Meetings that start and end on time, with a clear agenda and purpose, are less likely to derail your productivity. Some workplaces even implement \"No Meeting Days\" to allow for uninterrupted deep work. These strategies can help you manage meetings proactively, minimizing their impact on your valuable focused time.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0006-71-11918:7": 0.92086261510849, "clueweb22-en0042-76-04432:2": 0.8389133214950562, "clueweb22-en0042-76-04432:3": 0.8329358100891113, 