{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "That's a very common challenge when you have ADHD and experience hyperfocus \u2013 it's incredibly valuable, and interruptions like meetings can be jarring. Here are a few strategies to help manage meetings as a distraction:\n\nFirst, to protect your hyperfocus, try to **transition out of it gradually** before a meeting. You can set a timer, perhaps 15 minutes before the meeting starts, to gently shift your mental gears and prepare to switch tasks.\n\nTo minimize the meeting's disruptive impact:\n*   **Schedule Focus Time:** If possible, block out dedicated \"focus time\" in your calendar and set your status to \"Do Not Disturb\" to signal when you're in deep work. Some workplaces even implement \"meeting-free days\" to allow for more uninterrupted work.\n*   **Manage Distractions During the Meeting:** Maximize your computer windows to hide other applications, turn off email pop-ups and notifications, and put your phone on do-not-disturb or out of reach. If possible, use noise-canceling headphones or find a quiet space for the meeting.\n*   **Advocate for Shorter Meetings and Breaks:** If you have influence, suggest limiting meeting duration to 30 minutes or less, as sustained concentration can lead to fatigue. Also, if you have back-to-back meetings, advocate for short buffer breaks in between to allow your brain to reset.\n*   **Reduce Visual Overload:** In virtual meetings, if not essential, consider turning off camera feeds (yours or others') to reduce visual distractions and allow you to focus more on the discussion.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 0.48819172382354736, "clueweb22-en0013-52-18694:4": 0.5240107178688049, "clueweb22-en0041-38-03890:11": 0.48189952969551086, "clueweb22-en0000-56-03635:8": 0.7158480882644653, "clueweb22-en0000-56-03635:9": 0.586668848991394, "clueweb22-en0041-12-03522: