{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Since you ride a mountain bike on rough paths and in rainy weather, and comfort for your hands is key, focusing on your handlebar setup and grips is a great idea.\n\nFor grips, look for ergonomic designs with features like special grooves or a \"mini-wing\" shape that can reduce hand numbness, pain, and sore wrists by supporting your hand in an ideal position. Soft rubber and a tapered inner core provide shock absorption, which is crucial on rough terrain. Anti-slip properties, like moisture channels or textured surfaces, are important, especially since you ride in the rain. Lock-on grips are also beneficial to prevent them from slipping on the handlebars.\n\nRegarding the handlebars themselves, their width, rise, and sweep significantly impact comfort. Wider bars can offer better control and balance on rough terrain, while a suitable sweep can position your wrists more comfortably. Riser handlebars can provide a more upright posture, taking some weight off your hands. Consider handlebars made of carbon, as they are known to absorb more vibration than aluminum, further enhancing hand comfort on bumpy trails. Adjusting the handlebar height and stem angle can also fine-tune your riding posture for optimal comfort.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0025-95-17314:7": 0.6600411534309387, "clueweb22-en0004-98-08500:1": 0.6555467247962952, "clueweb22-en0023-62-08521:2": 0.5731801986694336, "clueweb22-en0035-81-05839:5": 0.5631012916564941, "clueweb22-en0036-65-06862:1": 0.5243504047393799, "clueweb22-en0035-81-05839:6": 0.4986814558506012, "clueweb22-en0015-57-16098:7": 0.4907790720462799, "clueweb22-en0015-57-16098:6": 0.490387499332428, "clueweb22-en0001-50-03380:15": 0.4865257441997528, "clueweb22-en0018-79-00311:2": 0.4844532907009125, "clueweb22-en0023-62-08521:1": 0.4613039791584015, "clueweb22-en0022-64-05972:3": 0.455077