{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-base-trained-MiniLM", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To fix your bike chain from repeatedly coming off, here's a DIY guide:\n\nFirst, thoroughly clean and lubricate your chain and gears. Grease and grime clumps can dislodge your chain. Use a brush with biodegradable solvent or degreaser, running it between gears as you pedal. Wipe away visible grime on the derailleur and chainrings. After cleaning, apply bike-specific lubricant, wiping off excess. (Passages 1, 2, 13, 19)\n\nIf the chain consistently falls off in the same direction, especially when shifting to extreme gears, adjust your derailleur's limit screws. Both front and back derailleurs have \"H\" (High) and \"L\" (Low) screws. Turn the \"H\" screw clockwise to prevent the chain from moving too far right (away from the bike). Turn the \"L\" screw clockwise to prevent it from moving too far left (into the wheel or between the seat tube and chainring). Adjust them so the derailleur aligns in the middle of the gear. (Passages 3, 14)\n\nNext, check your chain for wear. Measure 12 links; if the distance between the pins is more than 12 1/8 inches (30.80 cm), the chain is stretched and needs replacement. Worn-out or bent links also require replacement. (Passages 3, 9)\n\nYou'll likely need a universal chain tool for link removal or replacement. A bike stand or propping the bike upside down will make the job easier. (Passages 9, 18, 20)\n\nIf these steps don't resolve the issue, consider that incorrect cable tension on your rear derailleur can cause the chain to skip, potentially leading to it falling off. Adjusting cable tension might be necessary. (Passage 12)", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:11": 0.9380493760108948, "clueweb22-en0018-88-19973:1": 0.9229886531829834, "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:19": 0.9098073244094849, "clueweb22-en0022-62-09551:1": 0.8884627819061279, "clueweb22-en0010-76-17069:2": 0.872801